}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-3x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-3x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f2/f20c6478af528b45ab2ea7dfded3e835c49a79178e11d7dd06bb13760874bbdb.png)" 13194139534453 17592186045417 true] [17592186422156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422124 13194139534453 17592186045417 true] [17592186422156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422126 13194139534453 17592186045417 true] [17592186422156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422134 13194139534453 17592186045417 true] [17592186422156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422135 13194139534453 17592186045417 true] [17592186422156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422136 13194139534453 17592186045417 true] [17592186422156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422138 13194139534453 17592186045417 true] [17592186422156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422146 13194139534453 17592186045417 true] [17592186422156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422147 13194139534453 17592186045417 true] [17592186422156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422148 13194139534453 17592186045417 true] [17592186422156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422150 13194139534453 17592186045417 true] [17592186422156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422151 13194139534453 17592186045417 true] [17592186422156 1069 "q-333600" 13194139534453 17592186045417 true] [17592186422156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534453 17592186045417 true] [17592186422156 1071 "![](images/45/450d43a4cbf6dad8d54edbb66fc4a6e44e491290e1dc5d1303057fb0eb7928a7.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. \nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}.\n\nFinally, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector {{field-9}} as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is \n{{field-10}}.\n\nTherefore,\n{{field-11}} \n$>0.$" 13194139534453 17592186045417 true] [17592186422156 1072 #uuid "a8dac9e3-6d64-5716-8812-6c8cce0790e7" 13194139534453 17592186045417 true] [17592186422156 1074 "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/2d/2dace7c4f02fa0575d70264f33805d15470fa0894622c4d26d80fc1221aa2c54.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/40/40f0bf749448807b1a3aa2b593ae2e30acf26fabedbdc45da75e338e84c328f2.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are positive. The dominant eigenvalue is the one with the greatest magnitude.\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{2}$ as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, ${\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{1}>0.$" 13194139534453 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-017b9ef2-69f3-5607-98dd-c04ffd4f5965" 17592186422017 "answer-044a0690-55ba-5c10-a9ce-6daf70b82583" 17592186422018 "answer-05656c9c-b1db-5307-aacd-fa093ec114fb" 17592186422019 "answer-065efdb1-4ae6-597a-a0e1-aae8bc0611d8" 17592186422020 "answer-0c7c5dd4-985b-5405-bb39-51253042d52c" 17592186422021 "answer-0c90deb3-1281-549a-b9ec-dd098c7da22e" 17592186422022 "answer-116aa627-74f3-55db-9c3e-276499470af8" 17592186422023 "answer-11902238-9d9c-